      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-1",
      "name": "What is Dakhil Kharij and why is it called Namantaran in Lucknow?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Dakhil Kharij is the legal process of updating government land records to reflect a new owner after sale, inheritance, gift, or partition. Dakhil means to enter, Kharij means to remove. In Lucknow and UP it is also called Namantaran or land mutation. Governed by Section 34-35 of UP Revenue Code 2006. Administered by Tehsildar for rural land, LDA for LDA properties, and LMC for urban properties."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-2",
      "name": "Is Dakhil Kharij mandatory after registry in Lucknow?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, Dakhil Kharij is practically mandatory even though registry alone makes the sale legally valid. Without mutation the previous owner continues to appear in Khatauni and property tax records. Blocks home loan refinancing, utility transfers, resale, and compensation for acquisition. Banks treat Khatauni name as working owner record. Always complete mutation within 45 days of registry."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-3",
      "name": "How long does Dakhil Kharij take in Lucknow?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Standard Dakhil Kharij timeline is 30-45 days for rural land through UP Bhulekh. Complex cases with objections can extend to 90 days. LDA property mutations take 30-60 days. LMC mutations for urban non-LDA properties are faster at 15-30 days. Filing 30-45 days after registry avoids delays."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-4",
      "name": "What is the fee for Dakhil Kharij in UP?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Rural mutation fees in UP typically range Rs.200 to Rs.2,500 through UP Bhulekh. Some schedules list Rs.10,000 for sale deed mutations and Rs.5,000 for inheritance mutations. LDA transfer fees are scheme-specific. LMC charges nominal fees around Rs.500. Confirm current fee with concerned office before paying. Payment via UPI, net banking, or debit/credit card."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-5",
      "name": "Can I apply for Dakhil Kharij online for Lucknow property?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, rural and agricultural land mutation can be applied online through upbhulekh.gov.in. Application, document upload, fee payment, and status tracking are digital. For LDA properties apply at LDA office Gomti Nagar. For non-LDA urban properties LMC zonal office handles mutation. Revenue Court Management System at vaad.up.nic.in used by officials for case tracking."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-6",
      "name": "What is the difference between registry and Dakhil Kharij?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Registry is the legal transfer of ownership at Sub-Registrar Office through IGRSUP with 7 percent stamp duty and 1 percent registration fee, producing registered sale deed. Dakhil Kharij is administrative follow-up that updates Khatauni, LDA records, or LMC property tax register. Both required. Registry proves sale happened. Mutation makes it reflect in state revenue records that banks and utility providers check."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-7",
      "name": "What documents are needed for inheritance-based mutation in Lucknow?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Inheritance mutation requires death certificate, legal heir or succession certificate from court, family tree certified by Lekhpal or Gram Pradhan, Aadhaar and PAN of all heirs, NOC from all heirs if mutation in one name, previous Khatauni extract, affidavit of relationship with deceased. Missing documents is largest cause of rejection."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "@id": "https://dsdproperties.in/blog/dakhil-kharij-lucknow-mutation-process-guide#faq-8",
      "name": "Can banks reject a home loan if Dakhil Kharij is not complete?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, most banks require Khatauni or municipal record to show applicant as current owner before sanctioning home loan, home improvement loan, or loan against property. Pending mutation means Khatauni shows previous owner, causing rejection or delays. Some banks accept filing receipt as interim proof but final disbursement requires completed mutation. Common reason property loans stall in Lucknow."
      }
    }
  ]
